emerge --pretend: no cyclic GC during the run, no heap teardown at exit

Sub-series: D (garbage collector) - patch 2 of 2; applies to 3.0.83 with only the earlier patches of this sub-series.
Ported to upstream 73646c35d: context only (the emerge_main hunk follows upstream's new verbose_level() lines)

An `emerge --pretend` process only computes and prints a merge list. When
the process is the emerge program itself and the action is a plain
--pretend, the cyclic garbage collector is switched off for the whole run
(reference counting still frees everything that is not in a cycle);
_backtrack_depgraph collects once between attempts when more than six
million net container allocations have piled up, which bounds the memory
held by the graphs of failed attempts. After run_action() has returned, an
atexit hook runs the other exit hooks, flushes stdout and stderr and ends
the process with os._exit(), skipping the heap teardown of about a second.
It stands back when the return value is not a plain status, when an
exception was reported, or when flushing fails. The literal threshold of
patch 0009 becomes a named constant; other callers keep the 0009
behaviour.

On top of patches 0001-0018, resolve seconds before -> after
(median of the runs, at least 2 per figure):
  start 28.0 -> 26.9, holds 13.9 -> 13.6, masks 30.7 -> 28.7,
  py313 16.9 -> 17.0, bdeps 20.1 -> 19.7, policykit 14.3 -> 13.9
  sum of six 123.9 -> 119.8 (-4.1 s, -0.9 % of the stock sum 447.5)
Patches 0001-0019 together: 447.5 -> 119.8 s (-73 %); 447.5 is the
stock sum of the ladder run of 2026-11-20, stock was not run again here.

Kept as its own patch whatever its size: it changes process behaviour
(collector off, os._exit) instead of the resolver, and a reviewer may want
to take or leave it alone. Its step depends on what precedes it: about 11 s
in the first order that was measured (chain-prelim, single runs, before the
parser and constructor patches) and about 7 s in the second (chain-prelim2,
five states). Presumably the collector has less to do once the other patches
create fewer container objects; that was not verified.

Weak spots and what was not checked:
* Peak memory of a --pretend run rises (no collection below six million
  allocations). os._exit() skips interpreter teardown: anything that relied
  on destructors or on atexit hooks registered after this one would not run.
  Applies only when __main__ is the emerge script.

Origin: written by AI agents (Claude) in the emerge-fast job, 2026-11-17..20;
isolated from the full patch and measured on 2026-11-20..21. Not a human-written
change. Base: Portage 3.0.83. Test: `emerge -p -uND @world` on six states of
one system image, one resolve at a time; every run gave the same merge list
in the same order, the same number of backtracking attempts and the same
package count as unpatched 3.0.83.
